1. Definitions

1.1 “Licensor” means NanoDNA Studios.

1.2 “Software” means the original software and other materials owned by the Licensor and identified as covered by this license, including source code, object code, documentation, examples, scripts, configuration files, and original assets.

“Software” does not include third-party software, assets, or other materials that are separately identified or licensed. Third-party materials remain subject to their own license terms.

1.3 “Project” means the product, application, service, internal system, or other activity that uses, incorporates, distributes, or is operated using the Software.

1.4 “Business” means a company, corporation, partnership, or other for-profit legal entity, and includes a Project owned, controlled, or operated by such an entity.

1.5 “Related Entity” means an entity that controls, is controlled by, or is under common control with a Business. “Control” includes direct or indirect ownership or other power to direct the entity’s management or operations.

1.6 “People” means employees and contractors working for or on behalf of the Business or its Related Entities. Part-time employees and contractors count as one person each; they are not prorated based on hours worked.

1.7 “Project Revenue” means gross revenue received during the preceding rolling twelve-month period that is attributable to the Project, including revenue from sales, subscriptions, paid features, advertising, sponsorships, or paid services connected with the Project. Project Revenue is calculated before expenses and excludes sales taxes collected for remittance and amounts refunded to customers.

1.8 “Qualifying Small Business” means a Business that:

a. has no more than 20 People when counted across the Business and its Related Entities; and

b. operates a Project with no more than USD $10,000 in Project Revenue during the preceding rolling twelve-month period.

A Business qualifies only while it meets both conditions.

1.9 “Institutional User” means a nonprofit organization, school, college, university, research institution, researcher acting on behalf of an institution, government agency, or other public-sector organization.

1.10 “You” means the individual or entity exercising rights under this license.

2. Community License Grant

Subject to this license, the Licensor grants You a worldwide, non-exclusive, royalty-free license to use, reproduce, modify, create derivative works of, display, perform, and distribute the Software if You are:

a. an individual using the Software for personal, hobby, educational, or research purposes outside the work or operations of a Business or Institutional User; or

b. a Qualifying Small Business.

These rights include publishing and distributing applications or products that use the Software, including for payment, provided that You continue to meet the applicable eligibility conditions and comply with this license.

This grant does not authorize use on behalf of a Business that exceeds either threshold in Section 3, or by an Institutional User without the separate authorization described in Section 4.

3. Business and Project Thresholds

A Business must obtain a separate commercial license if either of the following conditions is met:

a. the Business and its Related Entities have more than 20 People; or

b. the Project has more than USD $10,000 in Project Revenue during the preceding rolling twelve-month period.

The People threshold applies to the Business and its Related Entities as a whole, not only to the team working on the Project. The Project Revenue threshold applies to the Project, not to the Business’s unrelated projects.

A Business that meets either threshold is not a Qualifying Small Business, even if it remains below the other threshold.

4. Institutional Users

An Institutional User must contact the Licensor and obtain written licensing authorization before using the Software. The Licensor may decide case by case whether the authorization is free or requires payment, and may set additional conditions in writing.

An Institutional User that contacts the Licensor to request authorization may use the Software while its request is awaiting a response and while the parties are actively negotiating in good faith, subject to the conditions in Section 5.

5. Transition to Commercial Licensing

5.1 A Business that becomes subject to Section 3 may continue using the Software for 30 calendar days beginning on the date it first exceeds either threshold. During that period, the Business must contact the Licensor in writing to request a commercial license.

5.2 If the Business contacts the Licensor within that 30-day period and the Licensor has not responded, the Business may continue using the Software while awaiting the Licensor’s response.

5.3 After the Licensor responds, the Business may continue using the Software while the parties are actively negotiating in good faith. This temporary permission ends when a commercial license is signed, either party states in writing that negotiations have ended, or the Business stops participating in negotiations.

5.4 If the Business does not contact the Licensor within the 30-day period, or temporary permission under Sections 5.2 or 5.3 ends without a commercial license, the Business must stop all use of the Software that requires a commercial license.

5.5 No commercial license is granted unless it is set out in a separate written agreement signed or otherwise expressly accepted by the Licensor.

